// @error WRITTEN THROUGH
// THE CONTAINER-FIELD EDGE of the alias closure (D823/D852).
//
// D821's `Writable` rule reads a per-VALUE alias closure that grows over three edges, all
// of which carry a list from one NAME to another. This program has neither end of them:
// `{ xs: b }` puts the list INSIDE a struct and `bx.xs` takes it back out under a name the
// closure never saw. The claim pass declined (`2` = unknown), the emitter fell back to
// master's rep-scoped answer, declined the widening, and the module the engine got was
// invalid — `vl check` rc 0 and `type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)`.
// It was D823's filed boundary cell, `d821_escape_push_alias`.
//
// The edge keys on the CONTAINER'S BINDING NAME paired with the field name, both read from
// the arena: this analysis runs while the check is still walking the program, so a type
// key silently matched nothing (a later handle's annotation is not resolved yet), and a
// FIELD-NAME-ONLY key reddened `type Other = { xs: i32[] }` with `oz.xs.push(2)` in it — a
// program that runs. The container's name must be bound exactly once and never assigned,
// so it cannot stand for two containers, and a container delivered anywhere else is simply
// not matched: the answer stays the `2` it is today, which is master's behaviour and never
// a refusal.
//
// The write pass now also runs BEFORE the claim pass. A write whose receiver is a member of
// the closed set is a POSITIVE fact, and the claim pass's "unknown" was discarding it.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Box = { xs: Shape[] }

function f() {
  const a: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const b: Shape[] = a
  const bx: Box = { xs: b }
  const c = bx.xs
  c.push({ s: 3 })
  print(a.length)
  print(b.length)
}

f()
